<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/intermediate/neurotransmitters-and-drugs">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You already know the synapse: vesicles release transmitter, receptors open or close ion channels
    and trigger G-protein cascades, reuptake transporters clear the cleft. This chapter treats that
    machinery as a pharmacological control panel. Every psychoactive substance — caffeine, alcohol,
    Prozac, fentanyl — works by intervening at one of a handful of sites: synthesis, vesicular storage,
    release, the receptor itself, reuptake, or degradation. And every major transmitter system is not
    a "mood chemical" but a <em>projection system</em>: a small cluster of neurons in the brainstem or
    midbrain broadcasting to defined targets, which is why one molecule can gate attention, movement
    and mood at once.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the major systems and how drugs plug into them" widget="equationStep" config={neurotransCfg} />

  <h2>The workhorses: glutamate and GABA</h2>
  <p>
    Most synapses in the brain are neither dopaminergic nor serotonergic — they are glutamate
    (excitatory) and GABA (inhibitory), the fast traffic of the cortex. <strong>Glutamate</strong> acts
    through AMPA (fast depolarisation) and NMDA receptors, the latter a molecular coincidence
    detector: it opens only when glutamate is bound <em>and</em> the membrane is already depolarised
    (relieving its magnesium block), letting calcium in — the calcium trigger for the plasticity you
    will meet in the memory lesson. The dark side is <strong>excitotoxicity</strong>: stroke or trauma
    dumps glutamate, calcium floods neurons, and they die of overactivation — why NMDA blockade is
    neuroprotective in principle (and why ketamine, an NMDA antagonist, is both anaesthetic and, at
    sub-anaesthetic doses, a remarkably fast-acting antidepressant, one of the field's genuine recent
    breakthroughs). <strong>GABA</strong> is the mirror: the GABA-A receptor is a chloride channel, and
    its opening hyperpolarises neurons. Benzodiazepines illustrate drug design at its safest: they
    bind an <em>allosteric</em> site and merely increase how often the channel opens when GABA is
    present — an amplifier with a physiological ceiling, unlike barbiturates which can open the
    channel without GABA and therefore kill in overdose.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "alcohol is a depressant, so it depresses GABA." Alcohol's main
    acute action is the opposite direction: it <em>enhances</em> GABA-A inhibition and <em>suppresses</em>
    NMDA glutamate — overall brain activity falls, hence "depressant." The withdrawal danger follows
    directly: chronically inhibited neurons up-regulate excitatory machinery, so abrupt cessation
    produces rebound hyperexcitability — tremor, seizures, delirium tremens — which is why alcohol,
    uniquely among common social drugs, can kill on the way out.
  </p>

  <h2>Acetylcholine: the original transmitter</h2>
  <p>
    Loewi and Dale's chemical-transmission discoveries ran through acetylcholine, and it remains the
    pharmacological classic: the neuromuscular junction (nicotinic receptors — blocked by curare,
    over-stimulated by nerve agents that inhibit acetylcholinesterase), the parasympathetic system,
    and the basal forebrain projections to cortex and hippocampus that support attention and are
    progressively lost in Alzheimer's disease — the rationale (modest clinical yield) for
    acetylcholinesterase-inhibitor drugs like donepezil. Nicotine is the receptor pharmacology made
    addictive: agonist at nicotinic receptors, including on dopamine neurons' inputs, delivering
    arousal and reward simultaneously — dependence with a delivery device.
  </p>

  <h2>The monoamines: dopamine, serotonin, norepinephrine</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Dopamine</strong> comes from two midbrain clusters with different fates. The substantia
    nigra projects to striatum to enable movement: lose 60–80% of those neurons and Parkinson's
    motor symptoms emerge — treated by giving the precursor L-DOPA, which crosses the blood-brain
    barrier (dopamine itself cannot) and is converted by surviving neurons; carbidopa co-administered
    prevents peripheral conversion. The ventral tegmental area projects to nucleus accumbens and
    prefrontal cortex: reward prediction and incentive motivation. Wolfram Schultz's recordings made
    dopamine famous — these neurons fire to <em>unexpected</em> reward, shift their burst to the cue
    that predicts it, and pause when predicted reward fails: a live reward-prediction-error signal.
    Addictive drugs short-circuit this system by raising dopamine pharmacologically rather than
    informationally; the system recalibrates (receptor downregulation), which is the molecular face
    of tolerance. Berridge's wanting/liking dissociation sharpened the picture: dopamine drives
    <em>wanting</em> (motivational salience), opioid systems deliver <em>liking</em> (hedonic
    pleasure) — the dissociation explains addiction's cruel signature: compulsive seeking of a drug
    that no longer produces much pleasure.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Serotonin</strong> arises from the raphe nuclei, which project essentially everywhere.
    Its receptor portfolio (over a dozen subtypes) explains its breadth: mood, sleep, appetite, gut
    motility (95% of body serotonin is enteral, which is why SSRI side effects include nausea).
    <strong>SSRIs</strong> block the serotonin transporter, raising synaptic serotonin within hours —
    yet clinical improvement takes 2–6 weeks, the timing mismatch that killed the naive "low serotonin
    = sad" story and pushed research toward downstream neuroplasticity (BDNF, dendritic remodelling)
    as the real mechanism. Acute tryptophan-depletion experiments (lower serotonin transiently) drop
    mood mainly in people with depression histories or on SSRIs — serotonin modulates vulnerability,
    it is not the thermostat. <strong>Norepinephrine</strong>, from the locus coeruleus, broadcasts
    gain: novelty and stress phasic-fire it, tuning cortex between exploitation and exploration;
    amphetamine and methylphenidate raise catecholamine signalling (dopamine and norepinephrine) in
    prefrontal circuits, which is the stimulant treatment of ADHD — inverted-U dose response
    (too little inattention, too much inflexibility), one of psychopharmacology's cleanest
    quantitative stories.
  </p>

  <h2>Drug classes, mapped to mechanisms</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stimulants</strong> (caffeine, cocaine, amphetamine): caffeine is an adenosine
    antagonist — blocking the brain's own fatigue signal — while cocaine blocks monoamine reuptake
    and amphetamine both releases and reverses transporters. Euphoria and addiction risk scale with
    speed and height of the dopamine spike (smoked or injected routes hit in seconds).</li>
    <li><strong>Depressants</strong> (alcohol, benzodiazepines, barbiturates): GABA-A enhancement
    with NMDA suppression; anxiolysis at low dose, sedation at higher, respiratory arrest at
    overdose — and lethal withdrawal, as above.</li>
    <li><strong>Hallucinogens</strong> (LSD, psilocybin): agonists at serotonin 5-HT2A receptors,
    destabilising cortical hierarchies; the 2020s renaissance trials (psilocybin for depression,
    MDMA-assisted therapy for PTSD) show large effect sizes under controlled conditions, with
    set-and-setting as a genuine experimental variable.</li>
    <li><strong>Opioids</strong> (morphine, fentanyl, heroin): agonists at μ-opioid receptors,
    mimicking endorphins — analgesia plus euphoria plus respiratory depression, the last being the
    lethal one. Fentanyl's potency (~100× morphine) compresses the dose-error margin to
    micrograms; naloxone reverses overdose competitively at the receptor. Cannabis adds its own
    system: exogenous cannabinoids on the brain's endocannabinoid machinery, retrograde signalling
    that presynaptically dampens both glutamate and GABA release.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Neuropeptides and atypical messengers</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond the small-molecule transmitters sit the neuropeptides — short chains (substance P,
    enkephalins, neuropeptide Y, oxytocin, orexin) synthesised as larger precursors, packaged
    in dense-core vesicles, released at higher firing frequencies, and acting at GPCRs in a
    modulatory rather than fast-transmission mode. Their pharmacology matters at systems
    scale: the opioid peptides' receptors are the target of both endogenous analgesia and the
    opioid drugs; orexin's loss is narcolepsy (and orexin antagonists are the mechanistic
    success story of modern insomnia treatment); GLP-1 analogues — gut-peptide hormones
    repurposed — reshaped obesity and diabetes therapy in the 2020s and are entering
    psychiatric trials, the largest live test of peptidergic psychiatry. Nitric oxide is the
    atypical case: a gas, synthesised on demand from arginine by nitric-oxide synthase,
    diffusing through membranes without any receptor — a retrograde messenger for vasodilation
    and synaptic plasticity (the mechanism sildenafil exploits). The endocannabinoids
    (anandamide, 2-AG) are the other retrograde case: lipids synthesised postsynaptically that
    signal <em>backwards</em> onto presynaptic CB1 receptors, damping both glutamate and GABA
    release — the dimmer switch cannabis pharmacology hijacks.
  </p>

  <h2>Synaptic machinery as drug target</h2>
  <p>
    The release apparatus itself is pharmacology. Vesicular transporters (VMAT2) load
    monoamines into vesicles — reserpine depletes them (the historical depression model),
    amphetamine reverses the transporters; synaptotagmin's calcium sensing sets release
    probability, and botulinum toxin cleaves the SNARE complex — the most specific
    pharmacological intervention known, simultaneously weapon and clinical tool (dystonia,
    migraine). Presynaptic autoreceptors tune release (alpha-2 agonists damp norepinephrine —
    clonidine's mechanism); transporter blockade (SSRIs, cocaine, methylphenidate) extends
    transmitter residence in the cleft; and enzymatic degradation (acetylcholinesterase,
    monoamine oxidase) sets the floor — the MAO inhibitors were the first antidepressants,
    with the tyramine "cheese reaction" as their pharmacological signature. Each site is a
    design surface, and the selectivity problem — a transporter serves many circuits — is why
    clean molecular mechanisms still produce dirty clinical profiles.
  </p>

  <h2>Dose, route and the blood-brain barrier</h2>
  <p>
    Pharmacokinetics matter as much as targets. The blood-brain barrier — tight-junction
    endothelium with efflux pumps — admits lipophilic molecules under roughly 500 Da and
    excludes most of the rest, which is why dopamine must be sent as its precursor L-DOPA,
    why diacetylated heroin crosses faster than morphine, and why peripherally restricted
    antagonists (second-generation antihistamines kept out by P-glycoprotein) act on the body
    without sedating the brain. Route changes risk: smoked or injected drugs spike brain
    concentrations within seconds, and the temporal profile of that spike is what drives the
    addiction-learning signal; oral routes smooth it. Half-life shapes withdrawal: short-half-life
    agents (alcohol, short-acting benzodiazepines) produce sharper rebounds, and methadone's
    long half-life is precisely its therapeutic point in opioid substitution. None of this
    appears in receptor diagrams; all of it determines clinical effect.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Dose-response is the discipline's spine.</strong> The graded log dose-response curve has
    three parameters — potency (EC50), efficacy (Emax), slope — and the <strong>therapeutic index</strong>
    (roughly LD50/ED50 in classic toxicology) quantifies safety margin: diazepam's is enormous,
    alcohol's is around 10, and digoxin's is close to 2, which is why some drugs need plasma-level
    monitoring. Partial agonists (buprenorphine) occupy receptors with a ceiling effect — the
    pharmacological logic of opioid-substitution therapy. <strong>Receptor theory's deeper layer:</strong>
    agonists differ in intrinsic efficacy, inverse agonists silence constitutive activity, and biased
    agonists preferentially activate one G-protein pathway over β-arrestin — the current medicinal-
    chemistry frontier (biased μ-opioid agonists aim for analgesia without respiratory depression).
    <strong>The innovation drought is real:</strong> all major antidepressants and antipsychotics
    still work through molecular targets identified in the 1950s–70s (monoamine transporters, D2) —
    chlorpromazine and imipramine were serendipitous finds whose mechanisms were reverse-engineered;
    since then, refinements. The glutamate (ketamine) and psychedelic entries are the first new
    mechanism classes in decades, and both came from clinical observation, not target-first design.
    <strong>Open questions:</strong> why placebo analgesia is reversibly blocked by naloxone (expectation
    recruits the same opioid machinery); what determines why two patients respond differently to the
    same SSRI (pharmacogenomics has clinical guidelines but modest predictive power); whether
    neuroinflammation is a treatment target; and whether circuit-level stimulation (TMS, DBS) can do
    what molecular psychiatry could not — close the loop between the circuits you know from the
    physiology lessons and the molecules psychiatry prescribes.
  </p>
  <p>
    A closing synthesis: the transmitter systems are best read as a control architecture with
    distinct timescales. Fast transmission (glutamate, GABA, acetylcholine at the neuromuscular
    junction) is the millisecond computing layer; the monoamines are the seconds-to-minutes
    gain-control layer, setting the parameters within which fast circuits compute; peptides and
    steroids are the hours-to-lifespan configuration layer, tuning receptor densities and
    circuit excitability — and the layers interact (oestrogen modulates dopamine; sleep
    pressure gates adenosine), which is why single-molecule stories always underfit clinical
    reality. The drug case follows the same logic: acute intoxication acts on the fast and
    gain layers (its effects read directly), withdrawal reflects configuration-layer
    compensation (slow to build, slow to reverse), and chronic therapeutics (SSRIs,
    antipsychotics) work by pushing the configuration layer until circuits adapt — the biology
    behind "these medications take weeks." Hold the three timescales together and the
    otherwise baffling pharmacology of this chapter becomes systematic.
  </p>
  <p>
    One distinction completes the vocabulary: <strong>tolerance</strong> (repeated dosing
    produces smaller effects — receptor downregulation, metabolic induction), <strong>sensitisation</strong>
    (repeated dosing produces larger effects — the psychostimulant pattern, where the wanting
    system grows with use), and <strong>kindling</strong> (each withdrawal episode makes the
    next one worse — the neurological reason "one more relapse" is never physiologically free).
    These are different phenomena with different mechanisms that popular usage blurs into one
    word, "addiction" — and the distinctions matter clinically, because a tolerant patient
    needing more drug for pain is not an addicted one, while a sensitised one craving the drug
    without pleasure is.
  </p>

